    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) and **Seiya Suzuki** (75.03) anchor the lineup as our most efficient starts, both benefiting from highly favorable matchups against back-end rotation arms. **Mickey Moniak** is in a prime spot against Nick Martinez, where a +14% park factor and +19% SP skill advantage propel him to the top of our rankings. Similarly, **Seiya Suzuki** draws Eric Lauer; while the lefty-lefty matchup typically dampens expectations, the weak SP_xERA of 5.20 and a +8% order benefit make him an elite play. **Tyler Soderstrom** also earns a strong recommendation (72.72) by taking advantage of a high-ceiling matchup against Lowder, whose 4.74 xERA provides a significant lift to **Tyler Soderstrom**'s base production.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was excluding **Cole Young** (36.38), who narrowly missed the cut. While **Cole Young** benefits from a +10% order boost assuming the leadoff spot, his raw projection was hampered by a difficult matchup against Drew Anderson (3.15 xERA) and negative park factors. Ultimately, we prioritized the plate discipline and matchup stability of **Travis Bazzana** and **Jose Caballero**, even though the latter faces a slight drag due to a lower spot in the order. We are also keeping a close eye on **Austin Riley** (51.86); he remains in the utility spot for now, but with a 33% rain risk in his contest against Eury P\u00e9rez, he represents our highest situational variance. \n\nManagers should note that we are still waiting on official lineups for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Josh Naylor**, **Brice Turang**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Max Muncy**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Corbin Carroll**, **Jose Caballero**, and **Austin Riley**. If any of these names are pushed down their respective orders, we will reassess our game-cap usage. Finally, while **Corbin Carroll** sits at a solid 67.66, his ceiling is slightly capped today by a matchup against Casey Mize, whose ability to hold runners (64% SB) 